I am working as a scrum master at eXtra (United Electronics). I need to create the developer shift schedule for the next week’s release so that all required project work is completed at the minimum total weekly cost.
We have 20 developers and 5 projects. Each developer has a different hourly rate, is eligible for only certain projects, and has additional availability and compatibility constraints.
The planning horizon is one week consisting of the following days: Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, and Saturday.
Each day has two shifts: Morning and Evening. Each shift lasts 8 hours.
A shift may have at most 3 developers assigned. A developer may work at most 1 shift per day. A shift is considered used if at least one developer is assigned.

Objective:
Minimize the total weekly cost, defined as:
Total cost = sum over all developers of (regularHours × hourlyRate + overtimeHours × 1.5 × hourlyRate) + 20 × number of used Morning shifts + 200 × number of used Evening shifts + total onboarding cost.
For each developer:

regularHours = min(total assigned hours, 24)
overtimeHours = max(total assigned hours − 24, 0)

Any hours beyond 24 per week are paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate.

Projects:
Project A (BNPL Integration) requires 32 hours. Project D must be completed fully before Project A can start.
Project B (Choicebox) requires 24 hours. Whenever Project B is assigned in any shift, that same shift must include at least one developer assigned to Project E.
Project C (Social Messaging) requires 40 hours. At least 24 hours of Project A must be completed before Project C can start.
Project D (Loyalty Program) requires 48 hours. It has no dependencies.
Project E (Payment Gateway) requires 64 hours. It has no dependencies.
Because each shift is 8 hours, the total required developer-shifts are:
Project A = 4 shifts, Project B = 3 shifts, Project C = 5 shifts, Project D = 6 shifts, Project E = 8 shifts.

Developers:
DA earns 20 per hour and can work on Projects A and B.
DB earns 22 per hour and can work on Projects B and C.
DC earns 24 per hour and can work on Projects C and D.
DD earns 25 per hour and can work on Projects A and D.
DE earns 26 per hour and can work on Projects A and C.
DF earns 28 per hour and can work on Projects B and D.
DG earns 30 per hour and can work on Projects A, B, and C.
DH earns 32 per hour and can work on Projects A, B, C, and E.
DI earns 40 per hour and can work only on Project E.
DJ earns 60 per hour and can work on all projects.
DK earns 85 per hour and can work on all projects.
DL earns 85 per hour and can work on all projects.
DM earns 23 per hour and can work on Projects A and D.
DN earns 24 per hour and can work on Projects B, C, and D.
DO earns 27 per hour and can work on Projects A, C, and E.
DP earns 29 per hour and can work on Projects B, D, and E.
DQ earns 31 per hour and can work on Projects A, B, and D.
DR earns 33 per hour and can work on Projects E, C, and D.
DS earns 36 per hour and can work on Projects A, B, C, and E.
DT earns 38 per hour and can work on Projects D and E.

Constraints:
Project E must be worked in exactly 5 distinct shifts, and at least 2 of those shifts must be Evening shifts.
Project D must be fully completed before Project A starts.
At least 24 hours of Project A must be completed before Project C starts.
If Project B is assigned in a shift, that same shift must include at least one developer working on Project E.
At most 3 developers can work in any shift.
Each developer can work at most 1 shift per day.
Each developer can work at most 5 shifts in the week.
Exactly one developer must work exactly 5 shifts during the week.
If a developer works an Evening shift on a given day, they cannot work the Morning shift on the following day.
In any shift, at most 2 developers can be assigned to the same project.
DA cannot work Evening shifts.
DL cannot work Morning shifts.
DJ and DK cannot work on Sunday.
DM is off on Monday and Friday.
DN cannot work Evening shifts and cannot work in the same shift as DB.
DO can work at most 3 shifts in the week.
DR is off on Wednesday and Thursday and cannot work in the same shift as DC.
If DS is assigned to Project E at any time during the week, then DS cannot be assigned to Project B anywhere in the week.
DC and DD cannot work in the same shift.
DB is off on Tuesday.
If DI works on Project E in a shift, at least one of DL, DJ, or DK must also work in that same shift.
Across the entire week, the number of distinct developers assigned to each project is limited as follows:
Projects A, B, C, and D may each use at most 2 distinct developers.
Project E may use at most 3 distinct developers.

Onboarding costs:
If a developer is assigned to a project at least once during the week, a one-time onboarding cost is incurred for that developer-project pair, regardless of how many shifts they later work on that project.
The costs are:
Project A costs 90 per developer, Project B costs 70, Project C costs 80, Project D costs 60, and Project E costs 120.
Exception:
If DT and DI are both assigned to Project E in the same shift at least once during the week, then the onboarding cost for the pair (DT, Project E) is waived.

Output format:
Return the final answer as a valid JSON object with two keys: "minimum_cost" and "schedule".
The value of "minimum_cost" must be a string representing the optimal total cost.
The value of "schedule" must map each day to its shifts. Each shift must include two lists: "Devs" and "Projects". These lists must have the same length, and each developer in the Devs list works on the corresponding project in the Projects list by position.

